{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $3.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $2{x}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=3.5\\cdot 2{x}^{3}=7{x}^{3}\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(16-0) \\\\  & =28\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "14\\,J"] ["math" "16\\,J"] ["math" "24\\,J"] ["math" "28\\,J"] ["math" "32\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163695" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "28\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,J"] ["math" "2\\,J"] ["math" "3\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163596" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2.5\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $2.5\\,kg$ at a rate of $(4-{x}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=2.5\\cdot (4-{x}^{2})=(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(10-2.5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[10x-\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(10(1)-\\frac{5}{6}({1}^{3}))-(10(0)-\\frac{5}{6}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =10-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{6}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "8\\,J"] ["math" "9\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163690" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{55}{6}\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,J"] ["math" "3\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=e.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $4\\,kg$ at a rate of $(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})\\,{m/s}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\nF=ma=4(\\frac{1}{x}+{x}^{3})=(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,N.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\frac{4}{x}+4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+{e}^{4}-(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{4}-0-1 \\\\  & =({e}^{4}+3)\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(2{e}^{4}+3)\\,J"] ["math" "({e}^{4}+1)\\,J"] ["math" "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J"] ["math" "4({e}^{4}+1)\\,J"] ["math" "4{e}^{4}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163693" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "({e}^{4}+3)\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $\\frac{8}{5}\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $3t\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{3}{2}({2}^{2})=6\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {6}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{5}-\\frac{9}{5} \\\\  & =27\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,J"] ["math" "16\\,J"] ["math" "24\\,J"] ["math" "27\\,J"] ["math" "36\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163714" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "27\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $2{e}^{-t}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =-2{e}^{-t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=-2{e}^{-0}+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2-2{e}^{-t}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=1\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)=2-2{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {(1)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,J"] ["math" "3\\,J"] ["math" "4\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163712" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity ${v}_{0},$ and final velocity ${v}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{6}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{6}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ and final velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=\\frac{1}{6}({1}^{3})=\\frac{1}{6}\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=\\frac{1}{6}({2}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\,J"] ["math" "4\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{6}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{4}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{21}{8}\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(1+{t}^{2})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=1\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=0+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=t+\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=1\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(1)=1+\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3})=\\frac{4}{3}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\frac{16}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{6}\\,J"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3}\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163708" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16}{3}\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude ${t}^{3}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{4}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{4}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)=\\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4})=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,J"] ["math" "18\\,J"] ["math" "24\\,J"] ["math" "32\\,J"] ["math" "36\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,J"] ["math" "12\\,J"] ["math" "16\\,J"] ["math" "4\\,J"] ["math" "8\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(3{t}^{2}+4)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=1\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=3{t}^{2}+4,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n1=(0)^{3}+4(0)+C\\,⟹\\,C=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}+4t+1.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=(1)^{3}+4(1)+1=6\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=(2)^{3}+4(2)+1=17\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {17}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\  & =289-36 \\\\  & =253\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "242\\,J"] ["math" "253\\,J"] ["math" "264\\,J"] ["math" "275\\,J"] ["math" "280\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163678" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "253\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=0\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2=\\sqrt{0}+0+C\\,⟹\\,C=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\sqrt{t}+t+2.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=\\sqrt{1}+1+2=4\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=\\sqrt{4}+4+2=8\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {8}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =192-48 \\\\  & =144\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "125\\,J"] ["math" "144\\,J"] ["math" "160\\,J"] ["math" "81\\,J"] ["math" "96\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "144\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $(t+1)\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=2\\,s$ and $t=4\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=t+1,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=2+1=3\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=4+1=5\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (5)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (3)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\,J"] ["math" "17\\,J"] ["math" "25\\,J"] ["math" "8\\,J"] ["math" "9\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163671" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\,J"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $8\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's acceleration is $(2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $t\\ge 1$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=2\\,m/s$ when $t=1\\,s,$ calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe acceleration of the particle is given by $a(t)=2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n2={1}^{2}+\\frac{1}{1}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{t}.$\nIn particular, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2,$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =81-16 \\\\  & =65\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "46\\,J"] ["math" "51\\,J"] ["math" "58\\,J"] ["math" "65\\,J"] ["math" "72\\,J"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "65\\,J"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $4\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $({x}^{3}-4)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=3$, and the particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=3$ to $x=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}({x}^{3}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-4x){|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{625}{4}-20)-(\\frac{81}{4}-12) \\\\  & =128\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=10\\,m/s$ at $x=5.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 128 & =200-2{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 2{v}_{0}^{2} & =72 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $6\\,m/s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,m/s"] ["math" "4\\,m/s"] ["math" "6\\,m/s"] ["math" "7\\,m/s"] ["math" "8\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163700" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters, the particle is initially at the origin, and the particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2,$ find the particle's change in velocity in moving from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8+4-0 \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=7\\,m/s$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=0$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {7}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{49}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=0$ is $5\\,m/s.$\n\nFinally, the particle's change in velocity from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}-{v}_{0}=7-5=2\\,m/s.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,m/s"] ["math" "3\\,m/s"] ["math" "4\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{4}\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\,m/s"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $6\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(8-x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(8-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[8x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(24-\\frac{9}{2})-(8-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at $x=1.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{1}$ at $x=3$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot {6}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =3{v}_{1}^{2}-108 \\\\ 3{v}_{1} & =120 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =40 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm \\sqrt{40} \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163694" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{2}{x}){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{1}{2})-(\\frac{1}{2}-2) \\\\  & =9\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ at $x=4.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {3}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s"] ["math" "3\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163696" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $7\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of the force of magnitude $(2x+3)\\,N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, the particle was initially at $x=1,$ and the particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3,$ calculate the particle's initial velocity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(9+9)-(1+3) \\\\  & =14\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ at $x=3.$ So, we can find the velocity ${v}_{0}$ at $x=1$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{1}{v}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot (2\\sqrt{5})^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 7\\cdot {v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 14 & =70-\\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2}{v}_{0}^{2} & =56 \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{0} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=1$ is $4\\,m/s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ca7306429494179cd3c59a0979613a89552f3f2ed6879cfc30b0f5e58a5ef8c"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4\\,m/s"] ["math" "4\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s"] ["math" "6\\,m/s"] ["math" "8\\,m/s"] ["math" "\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "q-163698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\,m/s"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/state.json" "file_sha256" "adba5108d0234859329c30363ab69e422d9561cab72413d97655d511d3e82a49"} {"question" "e-12525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12525.json" "file_sha256" "893e8f9085a828661d4bec9f649bac4ce6b9fb0cd4ab924ae135a36e53943e8b"} {"question" "e-12525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3+6{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3x+2{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(2)+2({2}^{3})-(3(0)+2({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =6+16-0 \\\\  & =22\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12525.json" "file_sha256" "893e8f9085a828661d4bec9f649bac4ce6b9fb0cd4ab924ae135a36e53943e8b"} {"question" "e-12526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x,$ and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12526.json" "file_sha256" "7a44a433e455e1fcb7ad1c84858f0fcf2b566984c722786474106775cd2db174"} {"question" "e-12526" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m,$ initial velocity $v_0,$ and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int (4t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =2{t}^{2}+t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0) = 0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = 2t^2 + t.$ In particular, its inital velocity at time $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and final velocity at time $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{0} & =v(1)=2({1}^{2})+1=3\\,m/s, \\\\ {v}_{1} & =v(2)=2({2}^{2})+2=10\\,m/s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ\\,\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {10}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =100-9 \\\\  & =91\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12526.json" "file_sha256" "7a44a433e455e1fcb7ad1c84858f0fcf2b566984c722786474106775cd2db174"} {"question" "e-12527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m},$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12527.json" "file_sha256" "c594a60e0168f039b84dacb5c6f501efb171e1d746e4b0d0ac8c95d55d3e275c"} {"question" "e-12527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by $x(t)=t^2-2t+3,$ where $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+3) \\\\  & =2t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) =  2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s},\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12527.json" "file_sha256" "c594a60e0168f039b84dacb5c6f501efb171e1d746e4b0d0ac8c95d55d3e275c"} {"question" "e-12528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2,$ calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12528.json" "file_sha256" "b71ad4df424707e343423bc9743111dcbbb28d5240046b3f91763b5145bee2f2"} {"question" "e-12528" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW\n= \n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3x]}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+12)-(2+6) \\\\  & =12\\,J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2.$ So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 12 & ={v}_{1}^{2}-4 \\\\ {v}_{1}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {v}_{1} & =\\pm 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration. \n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043252/example-12528.json" "file_sha256" "b71ad4df424707e343423bc9743111dcbbb28d5240046b3f91763b5145bee2f2"}] "usage" {"q-163601" [] "e-12527" [] "e-12528" [] "e-12526" [] "q-163596" [] "e-12525" []} "basis" 1606 "retractions" [[17592186180030 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $2x^{2}N$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x = 0$ to $x = 1$."] [17592186180034 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2tm/s$, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t = 1s$ and $t = 2s$."] [17592186179367 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis with an acceleration of magnitude $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ acting in the direction $OP$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2$."] [17592186179367 :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x=x_1$ to $x=x_2$ is given by\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nUsing Newton's second law of motion, the force required to accelerate a particle of mass $3\\,\\rm{kg}$ at a rate of $(1 + 2x^2)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$ is\n\n$$\nF = ma = 3 \\cdot (1 + 2x^2) = (3 + 6x^2) \\,\\rm{N}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the known values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{0}^{2}(3 + 6x^{2})dx \\\\\n&= [3x + 2x^{3}]_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= 3(2) + 2(2^{3}) - (3(0) + 2(0^{3})) \\\\\n&= 6 + 16 - 0 \\\\\n&= 22J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179368 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of magnitude $(4t+1)\\,\\rm{m/s}^2$, where the acceleration acts in the direction of increasing $x$, and $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the change in kinetic energy of the particle between the times $t = 1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t = 2\\,\\rm{s}$."] [17592186179368 :question/worked-solution "The change in kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$, initial velocity $v_0$, and final velocity $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\dfrac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= ∫(4t + 1)dt \\\\\nv(t) &= 2t^{2} + t + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0) = 0$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 2(0^2) + 0 + C \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad C = 0\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nv(t) = 2t^2 + t\n$$\n\nIn particular, its inital velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 1\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nand final velocity at time\n\n$$\nt = 2\\,\\rm{s}\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{0} &= v(1) = 2(1^{2}) + 1 = 3m/s, \\\\\nv_{1} &= v(2) = 2(2^{2}) + 2 = 10m/s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in kinetic energy of the particle between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔKE &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 10^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 - 9 \\\\\n&= 91J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179369 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $1\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The displacement of the particle is given by $(t^2-2t+3)\\,\\rm{m}$, where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,\\rm{s}$ and $t=3\\,\\rm{s}$."] [17592186179369 :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe displacement of the particle is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^2-2t+3\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is time in seconds.\nWe find the velocity $v(t)$ of the particle by differentiating the displacement with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2} - 2t + 3) \\\\\n&= 2t - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\nv_0 = v(1) = 2(1) -2 = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nand its final velocity, at $t=3$, is\n\n$$\nv_1 = v(3) = 2(3) -2= 4\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ΔKE \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot (4)^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 0^{2} \\\\\n&= 8 - 0 \\\\\n&= 8J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186179370 :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,\\rm{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(x+3)\\,\\rm{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x$. Given that $x$ is measured in meters, and the particle has velocity $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ at $x=2$, calculate the velocity of the particle at $x=4$."] [17592186179370 :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity.\n\nFirst, we calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=2$ to $x=4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= ∫_{x_{1}}^{x_{2}}F(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{2}^{4}(x + 3)dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + 3x]_{2}^{4} \\\\\n&= (8 + 12) - (2 + 6) \\\\\n&= 12J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle has velocity\n\n$$\nv=2\\,\\rm{m/s}\n$$\n\nat $x=2$. So, we can find the velocity $v_1$ at $x=4$ using the work-energy principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW &= \\frac{1}{2}mv_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}mv_{0}^{2} \\\\\n12 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot v_{1}^{2} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2^{2} \\\\\n12 &= v_{1}^{2} - 4 \\\\\nv_{1}^{2} &= 16 \\\\\nv_{1} &= ± 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can disregard the negative solution since the velocity and acceleration of the particle are positive in the regions under consideration.\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at $x=4$ is $4\\,\\rm{m/s}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186178181 17592186178182 17592186178183 17592186178184 17592186178185 17592186178205 17592186178206 17592186178207 17592186178208 17592186178209] "immutable_fields" [[17592186178180 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178180 :answer-field/id] [17592186178180 :answer-field/key] [17592186178180 :answer-field/type] [17592186178204 :answer-field/correct] [17592186178204 :answer-field/id] [17592186178204 :answer-field/key] [17592186178204 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
